{"availability":"available","full":"henryzhangpku/jevelin","name":"jevelin","description":"Fast, low-cost real-time agents: take small decisions off the critical path with a System One classifier (Jev). Live demo in your browser.","category":"AI & machine learning","language":"Python","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-09-22T21:48:52Z","updated":"2026-09-25T06:06:48Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://henryzhangpku.github.io/jevelin/","topics":["ai-agents","inference-cost","llm-agents","low-latency","pyodide","python","real-time","voice-ai"],"demo":"https://henryzhangpku.github.io/jevelin/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T09:23:41.328Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-08T09:23:41.328Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-08T09:23:41.328Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-06T07:21:26.129Z","stars":0,"forks":0},{"at":"2026-10-08T09:23:41.328Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["A real-time agent makes a dozen small decisions on every turn. Is this person reading out a card number? Do they want a human? Is this routine or hard? Is my draft reply safe to say? Most stacks send each one to an LLM, which puts them in the latency budget and the token bill of every turn.","jevelin is a working reference design for the other approach: take those decisions off the critical path. It answers them with a System One classifier such as Jev from TypeSafe AI, which returns typed, calibrated probabilities (no text) in a few hundred milliseconds. The classifier runs alongside the conversation instead of in front of it, and every turn is scheduled as a dependency graph, so time to first audio is simply its critical path.","These numbers are simulated from an illustrative latency and price profile, not measured on production traffic. The profile is a JSON file. Replace it with your own p50/p95 measurements and the same code gives your numbers. See What's real and what's simulated."],"features":[